Emscripten platform support in the C runtime template (bend2/comp.ts).
Every change is under #if defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__), so native builds emit
the same C and behave as before. The target is wasm32 with
-sALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH=0 and one worker (docs/decisions/0001 of bend-wasm).

- Term stack overflow: pool_stack maps the stack with a PROT_NONE guard
  page and catches SIGSEGV/SIGBUS on a sigaltstack; Emscripten's mprotect
  is a stub and wasm raises no signals, so an overflow would overwrite
  memory silently. Under Emscripten pool_stack maps no guard page and sets
  no signal handlers, and WL_ROOM (on native a check on the device only)
  stops before a frame would pass the stack's end with ERR_DEEP, the same
  "memory fault (machine stack overflow?)" as the native signal handler.
- Defaults (still overridable with -D): BEND_CORPUS_BYTES 512 MiB and
  BEND_STACK_BYTES 8 MiB. The corpus default comes from corpus_lay's
  check, cap = (span - HEAP_OFF) / (PAGE_LEN + 10) > CUBE, with span the
  corpus in words and the CPU's CUBE_LOG 7: ALC_OFF = 256 (for WL_RESW up
  to 96), RING_OFF = 256 + 16384 * 2 * 32 = 1,048,832, STAK_OFF = RING_OFF
  + 16384 * 1026 = 17,858,816, STAT_OFF = STAK_OFF + 16384 * 2048 =
  51,413,248, so HEAP_OFF = 51,413,248 + PAGE_UP(STAT_LEN) words. cap >
  16384 needs span - HEAP_OFF >= 16385 * 138 = 2,261,130 words, so span >=
  53,674,378 words plus the static image: 429,395,024 bytes (409.5 MiB)
  plus 8 bytes per static word. The next power of two, 512 MiB, holds
  static images up to 13.4 million words; 256 MiB fails the check. The
  layout does not depend on the worker count (CUBE_T lanes always).
  The stack default is 8 MiB, the stack of patch 0001's wasm32 run. The
  differential corpus does not bound it from below: every program that
  runs on wasm32 at 8 MiB (48 of 51) also runs at 512 bytes (2^9), and
  2^8 stops float_printing and fork_shared_flat with ERR_DEEP; no program
  of the corpus is deep. 8 MiB holds a million one-word frames
  (bend-wasm's probe/deep.bend pushes 20 million and overflows it).
- One worker: main forces the thread count to 1, and --threads above 1
  fails with "more than one thread is not supported on this target", so
  pool_open starts no pthread.
- corpus_grow fails at once, so a full corpus ends in the runtime's
  out-of-memory error (ERR_HEAP): memory growth is off and the corpus is
  mapped once.
- Unsupported effects fail loudly when reached, through err_fail:
  io_wait_on (every timer or stream wait that would lead to io_wait's
  blocking select), io_work (the helper pthread of files, processes and
  random numbers) and io_sys_addr (where every socket effect starts).
  Printing (io_out) is unchanged.

Retired by: an upstream Emscripten/wasm mode of Bend's C runtime
(https://github.com/bendlang/bend/issues/1598).

